# What takes the session back down, and the only thing that can.
#
# Stopping `rikka-session.target` is not enough on its own: everything waiting on a
# graphical session declares a dependency on `graphical-session.target`, and a target
# something active depends on is one `StopWhenUnneeded=` leaves standing. The desktop
# portal is exactly that -- `Requisite=graphical-session.target` -- so a session that
# only stopped its own target would leave the portal running and the machine still in a
# graphical session that has no screen behind it.
#
# `Conflicts=` is what says the opposite thing: starting this stops those, and stopping
# `graphical-session.target` stops in turn everything that is `PartOf=` it. rikka-compositor
# starts this with `replace-irreversibly` on the way out, so a job queued behind it
# cannot undo it. `StopWhenUnneeded=` then takes this one away again the moment it has
# done its work, leaving nothing behind for the next session to find.

[Unit]
Description=Shut down the Rikka session
Documentation=man:systemd.special(7)
DefaultDependencies=no
StopWhenUnneeded=true
Conflicts=graphical-session.target graphical-session-pre.target rikka-session.target
After=graphical-session.target graphical-session-pre.target rikka-session.target
